\section*{Limitations}

Small single-pass development benchmark (18 questions, 14 Type-A, 63 occurrences) with design-time exposure. Mapping ceiling: 14 of 63 occurrences can never receive direct credit, capping direct recall at 49/63. Evidence sparsity: 2508 of 2978 entities carry no candidate-specific evidence and 13558 of 20349 component instances are decided by the fixed midpoint imputation, under which an evidence-free candidate can outrank a weakly evidenced one. Both cross-encoders are uncalibrated and the 0.7/0.3 thresholds are frozen defaults, while tier 3 is strictly dominant, so one proxy error is expensive. The parser is heuristic: compounds can split imperfectly, problem phrases can be extracted ambiguously, and pre-head scientific modifiers remain verbatim in the original and structured representations without becoming separate positive components, so no complete semantic decomposition is claimed. Visibility is year-level; relation roles come from the supplied graph; all three models are general-purpose with domain mismatch. Single hardware, seed 42, no seed sweep and no confidence intervals, so arm differences are point values. Timing is mixed cold/cached, NLI determinism is untested, and the competition-rank routine is $O(mK^2)$ in pure Python, acceptable at $K=500$ and a scaling limitation beyond it.

\section*{Verified versus assumed}

\noindent\textbf{Verified by measurement in the frozen run:} every count and metric quoted above; hash verification of config, source, inputs, model manifest and prediction artefacts before and after evaluation; 0 denied reads, 0 gold reads, 0 historical evaluation reads over 4186 project reads; byte-identical cached replay of the prediction; determinism of the dense and global relevance paths on synthetic fixed inputs; exit code 0 for the prediction process.

\noindent\textbf{Assumed or conventional, declared in advance and not fitted:} the lexicographic criterion, the geometric mean and weakest-component terms, the neutral-midpoint imputation, the 0.7/0.3 thresholds, the four-tier semantics including positive-only tier 1, the pool size 500, evidence $k=3$, fusion $k=60$ and the channel caps.

\noindent\textbf{Explicitly not established:} that requirement-aware ranking improves conditional retrieval (here it does not, in aggregate); that any NLI status is correct; that a status change is a scientific improvement; that flat recall gains say anything about hierarchy benefit; that timings represent uncached or production cost; that non-gold entities ranked above a target are wrong. There was no human expert adjudication of any result.

\section*{Four more weeks}

(1) Calibrate or replace the support proxy with sampled human adjudication across all four tiers, focusing on \texttt{CONTRADICTED} precision, and only then consider softening tier 3 to a bounded penalty, declared in advance. (2) Run a declared sensitivity study of every A7 number against the imputation convention (midpoint, worst, drop-component), since 13558 component instances depend on it. (3) Declare and execute a matched hierarchy-versus-flat comparison with identical entities, evidence, scorers, snapshot, mapping and cost accounting. (4) Enlarge and re-annotate the benchmark with multiple annotators, explicit treatment of \texttt{PARTIAL} and \texttt{COMPOSITE} targets and a genuinely held-out split. (5) Seed and perturbation sweeps with intervals so arm differences carry uncertainty. (6) Engineering: an uncached timing run, an $O(mK\log K)$ rank routine, explicit NLI determinism tests, and review of the answer-family filter that currently excludes three dataset targets.

